    publishedAt: '2026-08-20',
    blackpaper: `Benjamin Bloom measured it in 1984: a student who gets one-on-one tutoring instead of a normal classroom performs two standard deviations better. That is the difference between an average student and a student in the 98th percentile. Bloom called this "the 2 sigma problem" because the tutoring worked and nobody could afford to give it to every child. One tutor per student does not scale. It never has.

The result is what the World Bank calls "learning poverty": the share of 10-year-olds in low- and middle-income countries who cannot read and understand a simple sentence. Before the pandemic that number was 57%. After two years of closed schools it rose to roughly 70%. Seven in ten children in the countries measured cannot do the single thing school is supposed to teach first.

UNESCO's Global Education Monitoring Report puts the number of undereducated children worldwide at 1.5 billion. That is not a rounding error inside the 2.4 billion school-age children on Earth, it is most of them. Layer on the adults who never got the schooling their labor market now requires, and the population affected runs into the billions.

The World Bank's meta-analyses of returns to schooling put the earnings cost at roughly 20% of lifetime income for a child who does not reach basic literacy and numeracy on schedule. Multiply a fifth of a lifetime of earnings across a billion and a half children and the number is not measured in millions or billions, it compounds across generations, because undereducated parents produce the next cohort of undereducated children.

The world spends about $6 trillion a year on education (UNESCO). That is not a funding problem in the naive sense, it already ranks among the largest expenditure categories on the planet, ahead of most countries' entire GDP. The problem is what the money buys: it mostly buys teacher salaries and classroom seats delivering the same fixed-ratio, one-to-thirty instruction that Bloom's research already showed is two standard deviations short of what is achievable. The $400 billion or so of that spend classified as EdTech (World Bank) is a rounding error on the total and has not moved the learning-poverty number in the direction it needed to move.

This is a solved problem in the narrow technical sense, we know exactly what works, and a completely unsolved problem in the delivery sense, because the thing that works has never been affordable at population scale. Every other quest on this site assumes a population capable of acquiring, applying, and building on explanations. This is the quest that determines whether that population exists.`,
    whitepaper: `The unlock is not a new pedagogical theory. Bloom's finding has stood for forty years. The unlock is that a large language model can now hold a patient, adaptive, infinitely repeatable one-on-one conversation with a student at a marginal cost near zero, which is the one input Bloom's method needed and never had.

What would actually have to be built: not a chatbot bolted onto a curriculum, but a tutoring system that (1) diagnoses what a specific student does and does not understand from their actual answers rather than a fixed lesson plan, (2) explains a concept multiple different ways until one lands, the way a good human tutor does, (3) holds enough state across sessions to know a student's history rather than starting cold every time, and (4) is honest when it does not know something rather than confidently wrong, which matters more in a tutoring context than almost any other AI application. Khan Academy's Khanmigo is the most visible attempt at this today; it is real but early, and the learning-poverty numbers have not moved yet, which is the honest caveat here, not a settled success story.

The buildable-now case rests on three things converging at the same time: LLMs cheap enough to run a full tutoring session for cents rather than dollars, enough evaluation infrastructure (learning-gain measurement, not just engagement metrics) to tell a tutor that works from one that just feels good, and enough device penetration in low- and middle-income countries (smartphones, not computers) to reach the population that needs it most, which was not true a decade ago and is true now.

**The market, worked from real numbers.** UNESCO puts the number of undereducated children at 1.5 billion (Global Education Monitoring Report, https://www.unesco.org/gem-report/en). The realistic price point is not the $6 trillion global education budget, that money is locked into salaries and buildings a tutoring product does not replace. The realistic comparable is Khan Academy's own Khanmigo enterprise pricing: districts buy in starting around $10 per student per year at scale (2026 pricing across multiple vendor comparisons), separate from a $4/month ($44/year) individual-family tier that is not the scale price point.

1,500,000,000 children x $10/student/year (district-scale AI tutoring, Khanmigo enterprise floor) = $15,000,000,000/year.

That is a $15 billion/year addressable market for AI tutoring specifically, not the $6 trillion education-spend headline this site's problem page cites for context, and not the individual-consumer $44/year tier, which would imply a larger but less realistic $66 billion figure that assumes every affected child's family can pay directly rather than a government or district paying at bulk rates. $15 billion/year is the conservative, defensible number: it is what it costs to put a real AI tutor, at real enterprise pricing, in front of every undereducated child on Earth, once, per year.

Two honest uncertainties. First, $10/student/year is a floor price observed in wealthy-country enterprise deals; delivering the same service in the lowest-income markets where the 1.5 billion figure is concentrated will likely require a lower price point or subsidized delivery, which would shrink the market-size figure and increase the capital-required figure simultaneously. Second, nobody has yet shown at national scale that an AI tutor moves a learning-poverty statistic, only that it moves individual learning-gain studies; the entire premise depends on that translating from lab-scale to population-scale, which is a real if, not a settled fact. Conjecture, open to refutation: the mechanism (Bloom's 2-sigma effect, now deliverable at near-zero marginal cost) is well established; the claim that deploying it changes the world number, is not yet proven and is exactly the kind of claim this site exists to track as it resolves.`,
