    publishedAt: '2026-08-20',
    blackpaper: `Malaria killed roughly 600,000 people in 2023, most of them children under five (WHO World Malaria Report, https://www.who.int/teams/global-malaria-programme/reports/world-malaria-report-2023). Tuberculosis kills approximately 1.3 million people a year (WHO Global TB Report). HIV/AIDS kills roughly 630,000 people a year despite antiretroviral therapy that, correctly delivered, suppresses the virus to undetectable and untransmissible levels. Add the three together and a preventable, curable, or suppressible set of infections kills on the order of 2.5 million people a year, every year, right now, in 2026.

WHO's Global Burden of Disease work puts the number of humans at risk of at least one of these three infections at 2.5 billion, roughly a third of the human population. This is not a rare disease problem. It is a distribution problem at civilizational scale.

The intervention economics are not in dispute. The Copenhagen Consensus, which exists specifically to rank development interventions by cost-effectiveness, places malaria bednets and TB/HIV treatment among the highest benefit-cost interventions ever measured: roughly $1 spent returns $20 to $100 in economic and welfare gains. GiveWell independently reaches the same conclusion and has directed hundreds of millions of donor dollars to exactly these interventions on that basis. This is, bluntly, the cheapest way known to prevent human death, and it has been the cheapest way known for over a decade.

And the death count has stalled. WHO's own series shows malaria deaths fell from roughly 900,000 in 2004 to a low near 560,000 in 2019, then rose again to 608,000 in 2022. Progress reversed. Insecticide and drug resistance are rising in the field faster than distribution can keep pace, and funding for the interventions that work has not kept up with population growth in the highest-burden regions.

This is the least intellectually contestable entry on this site. There is no debate about the science, no uncertain mechanism, no decade-away technology dependency. The bednets exist. The vaccines (RTS,S and R21) exist and are WHO-recommended. The antiretrovirals exist and cost, at negotiated Global Fund prices, under $45 a year per patient. The tuberculosis drug regimens exist. Every year this problem stays this size is a year the world had the tools and did not fully deploy them. That is the entire blackpaper: not a hard problem, a distribution failure, at a cost of 2.5 million lives a year.`,
    whitepaper: `There is no technology to invent here. The proposal is not a new drug or a new bednet, it is closing a known, quantifiable funding and delivery gap for interventions that already work, then holding that coverage against a resistance curve that keeps moving.

What would actually have to be built: procurement and last-mile delivery infrastructure at the scale of the Global Fund and Gavi, but with the coverage gaps closed rather than merely reduced; point-of-care diagnostics cheap enough (sub-dollar) to test-and-treat at the village level rather than requiring a lab visit; and, on a longer horizon, gene-drive vector control for the mosquito populations that carry malaria, which is technically closer than it was five years ago but still carries real ecological and regulatory uncertainty that has not been resolved. The tractable, buy-today layer (nets, drugs, vaccines, diagnostics) and the longer-horizon layer (gene drives, universal vaccines) should be funded and evaluated separately, because conflating them is how "someday" technology gets used to excuse not deploying "today" technology.

The unlock is price, not invention. Net prices have fallen from roughly $4-5 a net a decade ago to close to $2 today (Against Malaria Foundation, https://www.againstmalaria.com/DollarsPerNet.aspx), driven by manufacturing scale and competition among suppliers. First-line HIV treatment (the TLD regimen) fell from roughly $75 a person a year in 2017 to under $45 a person a year in 2023 through Global Fund procurement agreements (Global Fund, https://www.theglobalfund.org/en/news/2023/2023-08-30-global-fund-agreements-substantially-reduce-price-first-line-hiv-treatment-below-usd45-a-year). The interventions did not get better. They got cheap enough that full coverage is now a financing decision, not a technology bet.

**The market, worked from real numbers, disease by disease.**

Malaria: WHO counts 2.5 billion people at risk. A treated net covering one person for one year of protection runs in the range of $1.38 to $1.90 (systematic cost-effectiveness reviews of long-lasting insecticidal nets across multiple countries), call it roughly $2/person/year all-in with distribution. 2,500,000,000 x $2/person/year = $5,000,000,000/year to give every at-risk person a funded net-year of protection.

HIV: UNAIDS puts roughly 39.9 million people living with HIV worldwide. First-line generic antiretroviral therapy at Global Fund negotiated pricing runs under $45/patient/year (Global Fund, 2023 figure above). 39,900,000 x $45/patient/year = roughly $1,800,000,000/year to keep everyone currently living with HIV on suppressive treatment.

Tuberculosis: WHO's Global TB Report counts roughly 10.6 million new cases a year. Per-patient treatment costs vary enormously by setting, from roughly $30 for drugs alone in the cheapest low-income delivery contexts to well over $1,000 once diagnostics and case management are included in lower-middle-income settings (systematic review of TB care costs, PMC). Using a mid-range planning estimate of roughly $500/patient for a full low-income-country treatment course: 10,600,000 x $500/patient = roughly $5,300,000,000/year.

Sum the three: roughly $12 billion a year to fund full known-intervention coverage for malaria, HIV, and TB simultaneously, against the 2.5 million deaths a year the current coverage gap allows. That is the honest solving-it number, not the $200 billion/year figure this site's problem page cites for the entire global infectious-disease therapeutics and diagnostics market, which includes rich-world branded drugs, hospital care, and diseases well outside this scope. $12 billion/year is a rough, bottom-up, order-of-magnitude estimate built from real per-unit prices, not a market-sizing exercise, and it should be read as a floor: it excludes health-system strengthening, resistance monitoring, and the delivery infrastructure needed to actually reach the last mile, all of which cost more than the commodities themselves in the hardest-to-reach places. Conjecture, open to refutation: the per-unit prices are solid (cited, current), the aggregation across three diseases with very different delivery contexts is a simplification, and the real number is plausibly $15-25 billion once delivery infrastructure is priced in rather than assumed.`,
